  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 156 PM MDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nOur main weather concern this week remains hot, dry, and at times\nbreezy conditions resulting in elevated to near-critical fire \nweather, with all eyes then turning to this weekend's storm\nsystem. The previously noted trend in a quicker arrival to cooler\nair Friday remains on track, supporting cooler high temperatures\nin the 70s in the northwest CWA (especially Campbell and Harding \ncounties), ranging up into the upper 80s and lower 90s to the \nsoutheast. Forecast confidence is high in no precipitation until\nat least Friday evening, if not Saturday evening.\n\nLong-range deterministic models continue to key in on two primary\nfeatures this weekend. The first is a large low pressure center \ncrossing Canada to our north, which will assist in tightening the \npressure gradient and increasing northwesterly winds Friday \nafternoon and evening (gusts 25-40 MPH across the region). This \nwill especially be a fire weather concern...still not expecting \nWind Advisory-level winds at this time. Some hint of troughing \nextending southwestward from this parent low may also help support\nsome isolated shower/thunderstorm potential starting late Friday \nafternoon into the first part of Friday night, although confidence\nin this period is low (and NBM-based PoPs are trending a bit\nlower). Meaningful or widespread QPF is unlikely, if it rains at\nall. The second primary feature will be another closed low \nrotating down the back side of this Canadian parent low and then \nswinging eastward/inland across the northern Rockies. Moisture \nadvecting in on southwest flow ahead of this low, along with \nincreasing upper-level support, will bring us a better chance of \nmore organized precipitation sometime in the Saturday \nevening/Sunday/Monday timeframe, along with cooler temperatures\nand increased humidities. Looking at temperatures...the news isn't\ngreat for those looking for their first taste of fall, as NBM high\ntemperatures have moderated a bit...generally now holding in the\n70s Saturday, Sunday, and Monday (although some high temps as low\nas the 60s may still be possible up in the Black Hills and across\nthe northwestern South Dakota plains). Overnight lows will reach\nthe 40s. Looking at rainfall...forecast totals currently range\nfrom as low as 0.10\" across northwest Wyoming, to potentially \n0.50-0.75\" across the plains of central South Dakota. Continue to\nkeep in mind that our actual eventual temperatures (and QPF \namounts) will be tied to the strength and track of this low, and \n500mb height cluster analysis continues to highlight some \ndifferences/ensemble uncertainty tied to speed and progression of \nthe low (although so far no major trends affecting our forecast).\nOverall, no deviations were made from NBM and WPC guidance in the\nforecast from Friday onward at this time.",
